      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Trans-Pacific Partnership (TPP) is widely argued to have negatively impacted the domestic working class in the United States and other signatory nations, particularly through the offshoring of manufacturing and the collapsing of traditional union power. While some proponents claim it offered domestic policy wins or long-term benefits, the short- and medium-term impact on blue-collar workers has generally been a significant decline in wages and job security rather than a success. Consequently, most political and economic analyses conclude that the agreement was a net failure for the domestic working class and that \"America First\" rhetoric successfully framed it as such in the 2017 ratification debate.",
